{"kind":"artifact","text":"Chess ITEM 3, the public host surface, at exact head 8b5dd5ac2f9bd028e2d9aff5a94ad20d645e0be4 on request/chess-item3-public-api. The `host` package is the whole public contract a Go module outside this one imports to run on the gitseq kernel: Init binds a new sequence to an application permanently and records the binding as the log's first record; Open verifies the sequence and hands it back only to the application it is bound to, reporting an unverifiable chain ahead of any refusal to interpret; Append signs one act and gives it a position; Records returns the verified ordered records. There is deliberately no projection: an outside application owns its own fold. Every other package here is internal/, which the compiler enforces across the module boundary, so what this package exports is the entire surface. Proven by construction rather than assertion — host/external_test.go builds a real second Go module against this checkout and runs an application in it (TestApplicationInAnotherModuleRunsOnTheExportedSurface), proves the internal wall holds from the other side (TestAnotherModuleCannotReachInternalPackages), and proves the surface pulls in no application profile (TestPublicSurfaceDependsOnNoApplicationProfile). Gates at this exact head: gofmt -l empty, go build ./... clean, go vet ./... clean, go test -count=1 ./... green across all 27 packages including host and the docset citation gate.","body":{"commit":"8b5dd5ac2f9bd028e2d9aff5a94ad20d645e0be4","path":"host"}}